# Responsive Layout

The site is designed at three Figma breakpoints (**mobile 360 / tablet 768 / desktop 1366**) and the project owner has chosen the **fixed-breakpoint** pattern over fluid scaling: pixel values from Figma are hardcoded per breakpoint and we do not invent intermediate sizes. Above the desktop breakpoint the content sits centred in a **1366 px box** while the section background bleeds full-width.

## The two laws

1. **Pixel values from Figma are sacred.** Inside a breakpoint, every padding, gap, font-size, position, width and height comes from the corresponding Figma node. Do **not** wrap them in `clamp()` or `vw` to "soften the edges". The only exception is when the Figma design explicitly shows fluid behaviour (e.g. an offer rail that bleeds to the right edge of the viewport) — and then it is documented in the section's `constants.ts`.

2. **Section shell is always two layers.** Every section root looks like:

   ```tsx
   <section className="w-full bg-[var(--oczki-primary-100)] relative isolate">
     <div className="relative mx-auto w-full max-w-[1366px] flex flex-col items-start gap-7 px-4 pb-12 pt-20 md:items-center md:gap-8 md:px-20 md:py-24 lg:items-start lg:px-8">
       {/* layout, content, absolute decorations */}
     </div>
   </section>
   ```

   - **Outer `<section>`** holds `w-full`, the background (`bg-...` / `background-image: ... cover`), and nothing else that depends on content width
   - **Inner `<div>`** holds layout, padding, gap, alignment, **`max-w-[1366px]` and `mx-auto`**
   - **All absolute decorations** (wax stamp, notches, scallop frames, badges) live inside the inner so their positions stay relative to the 1366 box, not the viewport

## Why this pattern

- Above 1366 px the content stops growing and the section background extends naturally — no empty side gutters, just a continuous colour / texture / photograph that frames the cap
- Below 1366 px the layout is identical to the Figma source, with the same exact pixel values
- No "in-between" guessing — the team only QAs three states, not a continuum
- Easy to debug: every value has a Figma node it came from

## Concrete rules

1. **Cap is `1366 px`**, applied via `max-w-[1366px] mx-auto` on the inner div. Do not invent custom caps (1440, 1920, etc.) per section.

2. **Hero hero-photographs** keep `background-size: cover` on the outer `<section>` (or a positioned `<Image fill>` first child), so on ultra-wide displays the photo grows with the viewport. Other section types (cream, sage, textured) extend the background colour via the outer `<section>` `bg-...`.

3. **Tailwind breakpoints used**: `md` (768) for tablet, `lg` (1024) for desktop. Mobile is the unprefixed default. Do not introduce custom breakpoints unless absolutely necessary; the value `1366` only ever appears as `max-w-[1366px]` on inners.

4. **Absolute decorations are positioned with raw pixel offsets** taken from Figma. When Figma uses a constraint such as "centred horizontally", reproduce it directly with `left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2` so the decoration tracks the **inner 1366 cap**, not the viewport:

   ```tsx
   // ✅ wax stamp 1:1 from Figma node 6781:17291 / 7105:11469 / 7105:13764
   // Centred horizontally on every breakpoint, only the size + vertical
   // offset change between breakpoints.
   <div className="absolute left-1/2 -top-[57px] h-[112px] w-[112px] -translate-x-1/2 md:-top-[75px] md:h-[148px] md:w-[148px] lg:-top-[91px] lg:h-[180px] lg:w-[180px]" />
   ```

   Do **not** percentage-position decorations ("looks similar") unless Figma uses percentages, and do **not** anchor to viewport edges (`right-[…]` on the outer `<section>`) when Figma anchors to the content cap.

5. **Cards keep fixed width per breakpoint**, centred with `mx-auto` inside the rail. The width is the value from the matching Figma node (e.g. mobile card 328, tablet card 480, desktop card fills its grid column).

6. **Background textures** that need to span beyond the cap (e.g. paper grain) live on the outer `<section>` with `bg-[url(/figma/...)] bg-repeat` or a `radial-gradient`.

7. **Never** use `100vw`, `calc(100vw - …)` or viewport-based widths to make a rail extend to the screen edge. If a rail needs to bleed to the right of the cap, use a CSS variable holding the inner padding and reclaim it with negative margin **inside the inner** — that bleed terminates at the cap edge, not the viewport edge:

   ```tsx
   // inside the inner <div>
   <div className="px-[var(--offer-section-padding)] [--offer-section-padding:1rem] md:[--offer-section-padding:5rem]">
     <div className="w-[calc(100%+var(--offer-section-padding))]">{/* rail */}</div>
   </div>
   ```

## Smell tests

- Section background stops at 1366 px and shows the page body colour beyond it? → background is on the inner instead of the outer.
- Wax stamp / notch / scallop drifts away from the content on ultra-wide? → the decoration is in the outer instead of the inner.
- Card width changes between 1024 and 1366? → you used `clamp` instead of breakpoint-discrete `w-[…]`.
- Layout looks "ok-ish" at 1100 px but cramped? → you put a fluid `clamp` on something Figma defines as a discrete breakpoint value.

